## Simple by default. Advanced by need. Always.

Core product/design principle for **all** surfaces (member, organization/admin, forms, dashboards, navigation, workflows, settings, and documentation/implementation decisions). Full policy: `docs/04-operations/dev/AGENTS.md`.

Expose the smallest clear interface for the user's immediate goal. Do not display every capability merely because the system supports it. Prefer progressive disclosure, contextual intelligence, and plain language. Goal: **simple surface, advanced depth** — not fewer capabilities.

**Form fields:** Use outlined inputs with the label on the **upper-left border** (`OutlinedField`). When a signed-in member already has a value (name, phone, age, email, country), use it silently — do not show that field again unless it is missing. Standing rule: `.cursor/rules/form-fields.mdc` and AGENTS.md §3.

Icons for secondary/common actions (`+`, search, filter, more, edit, download, history) are preferred when recognizable, with accessible labels. Desktop hover may show a name; tap/click must work without hover. Unfamiliar or consequential actions keep visible text. Destructive actions must not rely on icon recognition alone.

Empty states stay minimal. If create is a compact `+` immediately beside the page title (accessible label; tap/click works without hover), do not duplicate a large Create button in the empty body. **More agreement types…** belongs inside the same `+` menu, not as a separate control.

Before adding a visible field, button, tab, filter, card, section, or explanatory block, ask: does the user need this now? Can Civizen infer it? Can it appear contextually? Can secondary detail be disclosed later? Is the simpler interface still clear to a first-time user? Is the deeper capability still discoverable?

Do not use this principle to remove important capabilities, accessibility, transparency, safety information, necessary user control, or necessary internal/reference documentation.

## After front-end or arc changes

Run:

```bash
npm run verify:post-dev
```

That sequence includes **`verify:home-post-composer`** (Home empty field click → focus → type), **`verify:home-happiness-shortcut`** (Home Score-card Happiness icon → `/happiness` @390px), **`verify:profile-score-dial`** (`/profile` Score dial after login), **`verify:happiness-foundation`** (`/happiness` @390px five-level check-in walk), **`verify:work-fulfillment`** (`/happiness/work` @390px Work Fulfillment walk), **`verify:fulfillment-plans`** (Improve Fulfillment Plan walk @390px), **`verify:wellbeing-aggregate-privacy`** (Happiness Privacy aggregate participation walk @390px), **`verify:wellbeing-insights`** (authorized Wellbeing Insights walk @390px), and **`verify:human-outcome-loop`** (Human Outcome Review walk @390px). Do not tell the user a UI fix is done until `verify:post-dev` passes **and** the change matches the relevant spec (not just scripts).

## Input-control replacements

Do **not** replace a working `textarea` / `input` with `contentEditable` or custom floated chrome for aesthetics unless the same session adds an automated **click → focus → type** proof and keeps a `verify:post-dev` runtime gate for primary surfaces. See AGENTS.md §3 **Input-control replacement gate** and the 2026-08 Home composer correction (broke on Testing v0.1.128 / `737086f`).

## After every push to GitHub

Run:

```bash
npm run verify:ci
```

Do not report a push complete until `verify:ci` exits 0 (CI green on `HEAD`). If it fails, fix and push again in the same session.

